WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:05.200
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.349
Reinforcement learning is a class of learning
algorithms that chooses a set of actions in

00:00:09.349 --> 00:00:12.140
an environment to maximize a score.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:18.260
Typical use-cases of this include writing
an AI to master video games or avoiding obstacles

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:21.750
with a drone and many more cool applications.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:26.380
What ties most of these ideas together is
that whenever we talk about reinforcement

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:31.109
learning, we typically mean teaching an agent
how to navigate in an environment.

00:00:31.109 --> 00:00:38.300
A few years ago, a really fun online app surfaced
that used a genetic algorithm to evolve the

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:44.910
morphology of a simple 2D car with the goal
of having it roll as far away from a starting

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:46.660
point as possible.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:51.899
It used a genetic algorithm that is quite
primitive compared to modern machine learning

00:00:51.899 --> 00:00:57.989
techniques, and yet it still does well on
this, so how about tasking a proper reinforcement

00:00:57.989 --> 00:01:01.720
learner to optimize the body of the agent?

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:09.010
What’s more, what if we would jointly learn
both the body and the navigation at the same

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:10.010
time?

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:12.590
Ok, so what does this mean in practice?

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:14.990
Let’s have a look at an example.

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:20.740
Here we have an ant that is supported by four
legs, each consisting of three parts that

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:23.580
are controlled by two motor joints.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:28.430
With the classical problem formulation, we
can teach this ant to use these joints to

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:34.570
learn to walk, but in the new formulation,
not only the movement, but the body morphology

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:36.500
is also subject to change.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:43.570
As a result, this ant learned that the body
can also be carried by longer, thinner legs

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:45.950
and adjusted itself accordingly.

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:51.290
As a plus, it also learned how to walk with
these new legs and this way, it was able to

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:54.090
outclass the original agent.

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:59.430
In this other example, the agent learns to
more efficiently navigate a flat terrain by

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:05.820
redesigning its legs that are now reminiscent
of small springs and uses them to skip its

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:07.220
way forward.

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:13.400
Of course, if we change the terrain, the design
of an effective agent also changes accordingly,

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:18.170
and the super interesting part here is that
it came up with an asymmetric design that

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:22.970
is able to climb stairs and travel uphill
efficiently.

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:24.000
Loving it!

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:29.340
We can also task this technique to minimize
the amount of building materials used to solve

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:35.570
a task, and subsequently, it builds an adorable
little agent with tiny legs that is still

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:38.960
able to efficiently traverse this flat terrain.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:43.440
This principle can also be applied to the
more difficult version of this terrain, which

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:49.200
results in a lean, insect-like solution that
can still finish this level that uses about

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.960
75% less materials than the original solution.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:58.650
And again, remember that not only the design,
but the movement is learned here at the same

00:02:58.650 --> 00:02:59.650
time.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:03.110
While we look at these really fun bloopers,
I’d like to let you know that we have an

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:09.370
opening at our Institute at the Vienna University
